{
	title: 'System-prompt change trail in the transcript',
	spec: 'When a file in a session\'s system prompt (SYSTEM.md, the files it includes, the AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md files for its cwd) changes, the session gets an output record with the change: file name (relative to the cwd when inside it), changed/added/removed, and a short diff (git diff -U1, headers dropped, at most 40 lines). The prompt-file watcher (task 48) runs the check for every open tab when it sees a save, and at host start for edits made while it was down; the first check of a session only remembers (state in the session\'s prompt-files.json), and files that come or go with a cwd change are not reported. The model reads each record as a <meta> note in front of its next prompt, saying the user edited its instructions and earlier replies followed the old text, with the diff, so it does not mistake new rules for ones it ignored. Clients show consecutive change records as one card: one change heads as \'SYSTEM.md changed\'; several as \'3 changes to SYSTEM.md, AGENTS.md since 10:44\' (a date before the time if not today). Both clients draw it alike, without a header: one change is one row ("11:25 AGENTS.md changed  +2 −0") with up to three changed lines in diff colours, or "blank lines only"; a run is one row ("11:14–11:25 3 changes to SYSTEM.md, AGENTS.md"; one time when first and last match) and one aligned row per change: time, file, counts, its first changed line. A change replacing one non-blank line with another shows, dim, its changed words with a word of context instead ("Apply Strunk → Prunk & …", "−Foo Pull …", "… end +now"). The web card opens on a click to each change\'s line and whole diff, in the same colours.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Records are output records with a change field (no new record type): replay turns them into notes, transcript.aside carries change, and promptChanges.group merges runs at draw time in both clients (web view.rows, terminal frame.layout), memoized per run so keyed rows and the terminal layout cache keep the object.'
